  "reasoning": "The patient presents with a combination of neurological, visual, and gastrointestinal symptoms: migraines, regular headaches, distorted eyesight, excessive appetite, painful neck, melancholy, irritability, acid reflux, and indigestion. While acid reflux and indigestion could point toward gastrointestinal conditions like gastroesophageal reflux disease or peptic ulcer disease, those diagnoses fail to explain the prominent visual distortions, excessive appetite, neck pain, and mood changes. Comparing the symptom cluster to the provided training references, Reference [1] describes nearly identical symptoms ('frequent headaches, stomach acid, indigestion, hazy and distorted vision, increased appetite, stiff neck, depression, irritation') and explicitly diagnoses them as migraine. Additional migraine references ([5], [11], [15], [18]) consistently link headaches with visual disruptions, mood alterations, and digestive upset. Therefore, migraine is the single diagnosis that comprehensively accounts for all the patient's symptoms.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]migraine[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
